Œ[{"role": "assistant", "parts": [{"type": "text", "content": "**The fastest total time is 17 minutes.**\n\n## Schedule\n\n| Step | Who crosses | Direction | Time | Elapsed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 and 2 | â†’ | 2 | 2 |\n| 2 | 1 (carries torch back) | â† | 1 | 3 |\n| 3 | 5 and 10 | â†’ | 10 | 13 |\n| 4 | 2 (carries torch back) | â† | 2 | 15 |\n| 5 | 1 and 2 | â†’ | 2 | 17 |\n\n## Why this is best\n\n- The obvious plan is to have the 1-minute person escort everyone: 2 + 1 + 5 + 1 + 10 = 19 minutes. It wastes time because the 5 and the 10 each get their own slow crossing.\n- The better idea is to send the two slowest together, so the 5-minute walk is hidden inside the 10-minute one. The two fastest people then act as torch shuttlers, one on each side of that slow crossing.\n\n**Lower-bound sketch:**\n1. The torch must come back, so any plan needs at least 5 crossings (3 forward, 2 back).\n2. If the 5 and 10 cross separately, those two crossings cost at least 15. The other three or more crossings cost at least 1 each, so the total is at least 18.\n3. So the 5 and 10 should cross together, which costs 10. If they cross first, one of them must walk back, adding at least 5, so the total is again at least 18.\n4. Otherwise the 1 and 2 cross first and one of them returns with the torch. After the 5 and 10 cross, the other one returns, and then the 1 and 2 cross together. This costs 2 + 1 + 10 + 2 + 2 = 17 (or 2 + 2 + 10 + 1 + 2 = 17 if the 2 returns first).\n\nSo 17 minutes is optimal.\n"}], "finish_reason": "end_turn"}]z…␀␀␀
	
